    in the unit suite.

  The [38;5;153mfields[39m-allowlist and sink-interface boundaries are what make all of this testable without a browser or a network,
  which is most of why the core is shaped that way.

  [1mRollout.[0m Three steps, each independently revertible:

  1. Land [38;5;153msrc/logging/[39m with the full test suite and no callers. Nothing in the app changes; nothing can break.
  2. Convert the two [38;5;153msrc/[39m files and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to ESM, wire [38;5;153mnode.js[39m into [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m. Node side first because it has no
     vendor, no network, and no privacy surface — it proves the core works with the cheapest possible blast radius.
  3. Wire [38;5;153mbrowser.js[39m into [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, replacing the three existing [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m calls, with [38;5;153mlevel: "warn"[39m and the vendor key in
     place.

  [1mTwo assumptions I'm carrying into the spec[0m, written as assumptions rather than facts since nobody has confirmed them:

  - [3mAssumption:[0m the chosen vendor offers a write-only, rotatable ingest key and an HTTP ingest endpoint usable without
    their SDK. Validate by checking the vendor's docs before step 3 — if it fails, the no-SDK decision from Section 1
    has to be revisited.
  - [3mAssumption:[0m sending anonymous session IDs and opaque user IDs to a third-party processor is acceptable under your
    privacy obligations. Validate with whoever owns that sign-off before step 3. Steps 1 and 2 don't depend on it, which
    is deliberate — the privacy review can run in parallel without blocking the work.

  I'd also note the vendor itself is still unpicked. The design is vendor-agnostic by construction, so it doesn't block
  the spec, but step 3 can't start until you name one.

  If Section 4 looks right, that's the whole design — I'll write it up to
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-26-logging-design.md[39m (uncommitted), run a self-review and the Codex spec gate over it,
  then hand it back to you to read before anything gets built.

